<clickhouse>
    <!--
      ClickHouse logs its own telemetry into system tables, and the image
      ships those tables with no TTL. On this host they reached 22.8 GiB
      against 3.3 GiB of actual trading data, filled a 79 GB disk, and took
      the stack down: the P&L job could not write a temp file (errno 28),
      Redpanda could not write a segment and restarted 238 times, and the
      ingestor went unhealthy behind it.

      Two of those tables earn nothing here and are removed outright:

      text_log            9.3 GiB — ClickHouse's own log lines, duplicated
                          into a table. The same lines are already in
                          `docker logs aware-clickhouse`. It arrives at
                          ~300k rows an hour, which is where most of the
                          disk went.
      processors_profile_log
                          6.9 GiB — per-processor query profiling. Useful
                          when tuning a slow query by hand, dead weight the
                          rest of the time.

      The rest are worth keeping — query_log is how you find a slow or
      failing query after the fact — but with a horizon. Three days covers
      any incident you would actually investigate.

      Changing a log table's TTL makes ClickHouse rename the existing one
      aside (query_log_0) rather than alter it, so the old data keeps its
      space until dropped. Drop the old tables once after deploying this;
      ClickHouse recreates them on the next flush.
    -->
    <text_log remove="1"/>
    <processors_profile_log remove="1"/>

    <query_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 3 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </query_log>
    <query_views_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 3 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </query_views_log>
    <trace_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 3 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </trace_log>
    <part_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 3 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </part_log>
    <metric_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 3 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </metric_log>
    <asynchronous_metric_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 3 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </asynchronous_metric_log>
    <error_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 3 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </error_log>
</clickhouse>
